====Usage notes====
* According to Google Ngram Viewer corpus data, as of 2019, ''naïveté'' and ''naivety'' were the most common spellings; ''naivety'' was the most common spelling in British English while ''naïveté'' was the most common spelling in American English.<ref name=gnv>{{R:GNV|naïveté, naivete, naivety, naïvety, naiveté, naiveness, naïveness}}</ref> ''naivete'' used to be the most common variant but dropped sharply after 2000.<ref name=gnv/> Whether the Viewer accurately tracks accents is unclear.
* Comparing the ''-ete'' forms and the ''-ety'' forms as two groups yields that in British English ''-ety'' forms are slightly more common while in American English the ''-ete'' forms are much more common.<ref>{{R:GNV|naïveté+naivete+naiveté, naivety+naïvety}}</ref>
* Spellings in dictionaries:
** ''naïveté'' is covered by Merriam-Webster (as a variant),<ref name=mw>{{R:MWO}}</ref> AHD,<ref name=ahd>{{R:AHD}}</ref> Collins (as a variant),<ref name=col>{{R:Collins}}</ref> OED<ref name=oed>{{R:OED Online|id=124884}}</ref> and Century 1911.<ref>{{R:Century 1911|naivete}}</ref>
** ''naivete'' is covered by Merriam-Webster.<ref name=mw/>
** ''naiveté'' is covered by Merriam-Webster (as less commonly),<ref name=mw/> AHD,<ref name=ahd/> Collins,<ref name=col/> Cambridge (as a variant),<ref name=cam/> and Macmillan American.<ref name=macm>{{R:Macmillan|naive}}</ref>
** ''naivety'' is covered by Macmillan British,<ref name=macm/> Cambridge<ref name=cam>{{R:Cambridge|naivety}}</ref> and OED.<ref>{{R:OED Online|id=124885|entry=naivety}}</ref>
* US Government Publishing Office manual states that "Diacritical marks are not used with anglicized words" and mentions ''naive'' and ''naivete''.<ref>{{R:GPOSM}}</ref>
* Guardian and Observer style guide indicates ''naive'', ''naively'', and ''naivety'' with no accent.<ref>[https://www.theguardian.com/styleguide Guardian and Observer style guide], theguardian.com</ref>
* The diaeresis in ''naïveté'' is there to indicate the vowel is pronounced in a separate syllable.<ref>[https://www.merriam-webster.com/words-at-play/mary-norris-diaeresis What's a Diaeresis? | Merriam-Webster]</ref>

